From a two-day process review to a year-long change programme, the same people stay on the engagement from first workshop to handover.

Every service starts with a short conversation about the problem, not the solution. We tell you early when a smaller project would do the job, and we say so when a service is not the right fit.

## What we are asked for most

- Operations and process reviews
- Software selection and vendor negotiation
- Reporting and management dashboards
- Succession and reorganisation support

## How long a project takes

| Service | Usual length | First result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Process review | 2 to 6 weeks | A map of the process and the three changes that matter most |
| Software selection | 2 to 4 months | A shortlist of three vendors with a scored demonstration |
| Reporting and dashboards | 4 to 10 weeks | One weekly report that the whole team reads |
| Succession support | 6 to 18 months | A written plan that the family and the managers agree on |

## How a typical project runs

A project usually has four phases: listening, designing, testing and handover. The listening phase takes one to three weeks, depending on the size of the team. The design phase produces a plan that the people who do the work have helped to shape. Testing is done on real cases, not on examples. The handover phase ends when your team can run the new way of working on its own.
